1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,760
Vasovagal syncope — that sudden blackout after donating blood or standing too long in the heat, turns out it's your vagus nerve overreacting, not your heart failing.

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,560
Here's how to catch the warning signs and stay safe.

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,600
Blood donation is the single most common trigger.

4
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:30,800
Red Cross donor data show fainting risk is highest in young, first-time donors — 16 and 17-year-olds faint at more than three times the rate of donors over 20, and more than half of all donor fainting reactions happen in people 22 or younger.

5
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:44,160
Vasovagal syncope also shows up with the sight of blood or needles, intense pain or fear, prolonged standing — think graduation ceremonies or long lines in the heat — and sudden emotional stress.

6
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:49,760
It happens because your vagus nerve, part of the parasympathetic system, overreacts to the trigger.

7
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:58,800
Your heart rate drops, your blood vessels suddenly widen, and blood pressure crashes, so less blood reaches your brain for a few seconds and you lose consciousness.

8
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,880
Four habits lower your risk.

9
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:10,800
Recognizing the warning signs and sitting or lying down right away, physical counterpressure moves, staying hydrated with enough salt, and planning ahead for known triggers.

10
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:13,760
First, act on the warning signs immediately.

11
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:21,760
If you feel cold sweats, tunnel vision, or lightheadedness, sit down right away or lie down with your legs raised above heart level.

12
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,640
That restores blood flow to your brain fast.

13
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,680
Second, use physical counterpressure maneuvers.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,840
Crossing your legs while tensing your thigh and calf muscles, or gripping your hands together and pulling them apart, raises your blood pressure within seconds.

15
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:47,920
In a randomized trial published in the Journal of the American College of Cardiology, people who practiced these moves had a recurrent fainting rate of 32%, compared to 51% in those who didn't.

16
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,440
Third, stay hydrated and don't skip salt.

17
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:55,840
More fluid and blood volume make your blood pressure much harder to crash.

18
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:01,440
If you're on a salt-restricted diet for another condition, talk to your doctor before changing your intake.

19
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:04,640
Finally, plan ahead for your known triggers.

20
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:13,360
Ask to give blood lying down instead of sitting up, and if you have to stand for a long time, shift your weight and pump your calves instead of staying still.

21
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:21,120
Recognizing the warning signs, practicing counterpressure moves, and staying hydrated genuinely cut your risk of fainting again.

22
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:25,920
If you've fainted more than once or gotten hurt from a fall, get an EKG.

23
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:32,080
And if you already have a diagnosed heart condition, don't manage this on your own — talk to your doctor first.
